<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oorlam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orlam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oorlam were groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> and mixed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> frontier who had adopted horses, firearms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch">Cape Dutch</a> speech and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. From the late 18th century bands such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> and the Witboois migrated north across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, where their mounted commandos dominated the Nama and raided Herero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. They gradually merged with the Nama, whose society they reshaped.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oorlam.jpg" alt="Oorlam" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Jonker_Afrikaner_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
